00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:09.948
INES ENVID: Sure.

00:05:09.948 --> 00:05:13.670
So I've been a
long-term technology

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:15.290
person working in networking.

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:18.570
So I've been doing a lot
of enterprise and carrier,

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.800
working from Access
to Edge to Backbone.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:26.100
And I've been transitioning
to a public cloud,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.600
and I'm so excited to be now
part of Google, where we're

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.100
building one of the most amazing
infrastructures and products

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:34.914
in the planet.

00:05:34.914 --> 00:05:35.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.480
So can you tell us a little bit,
so from a very, very high point

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.600
of view, what is networking?

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.770
Like, machines talking
together to each other,

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.385
but what do you do with
networking, really?

00:05:49.385 --> 00:05:51.010
INES ENVID: So yeah,
you have machines,

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.660
and that's a good
starting point,

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:57.920
but you cannot do much unless
you are able to actually give

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:58.920
them a channel to talk.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:03.460
So networking is all about
making that communication

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:08.040
efficient and flexible
so that you can actually

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.785
build topology that
is allowing, only

00:06:10.785 --> 00:06:13.340
the most primitive
types of communication

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.360
machine to machine,
but you're actually

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.770
able to build your multicast,
your broadcast environments.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:23.380
So it is a way of
providing the plumbing,

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:28.220
if you wish, for machines
so that we can actually

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:31.650
build richer applications
on top of them.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:32.400
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.450
So I guess there sounds
like there's probably

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.460
almost several
components to networking.

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.100
There's the hardware side
that runs in our data center

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.224
and then there's the software
side that probably we

00:06:41.224 --> 00:06:42.960
talk about as well as GCP.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.130
Why don't we start
the hardware side?

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:47.050
What makes our stuff good?

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.070
What's so special about
the network that we run on?

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:50.790
INES ENVID: Sure.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:56.930
So traditionally, networking has
been running in dedicated boxes

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:00.080
and everything was
really about having

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.980
dedicated ASICs, dedicated FPA.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.600
So it's very
hardware-oriented and that

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.370
build, in the last decades,
great technology, which

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:12.690
was deployed
on-premises, was deployed

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:14.530
at the edge and the backbones.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:22.160
And those machines, bridges,
routers, load balancers,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.620
they kept developing,
and of course,

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.260
the requirements on performance,
on functional capabilities just

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:30.290
are more and more demanding.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.160
But also as part of that
evolution, in the last year,

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.710
it has been very exciting,
all the transition about being

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:40.190
able to actually run those in
more, not dedicated hardware,

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:42.520
but more hardware
that is used for any

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.350
of the compute functions
today can actually

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:49.240
be used to build great
networking equipment.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:53.220
So together with the trend to
have a lot more distributed

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.270
environment, because
what allows, actually,

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:59.199
to have the performance is
that you are not bottlenecked

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:01.740
to a single box, where you would
actually have great hardware

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.040
to run it, but
then you can start

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:06.490
distributing those
functions, and then you

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:09.066
need a lot of logic
in order to do that.

00:08:09.066 --> 00:08:11.640
And what it makes Google
infrastructure great

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:15.685
is that it has been investing
on data center, which actually

00:08:15.685 --> 00:08:18.880
provide all the distribution
across the globe, where you are

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:23.470
actually be able to send
your traffic as needed

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:25.550
and distribute it
to the right points

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.660
closest to the
applications where

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:31.300
it makes more sense for
latency and for performance.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.070
And of course, Google
has been building

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:37.630
on that to deploy different
[? talents ?] and the back-ends

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:41.900
for all the search and YouTube
and all the infrastructure.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.100
So leveraging on that
is also a starting point

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.400
for building Cloud functions.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:50.770
So that is really, I
think, the best asset

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:52.600
the Google Cloud
is able to leverage

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:56.060
that great infrastructure and
provide the secure environment

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:59.500
for users to build their
applications the same way

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.340
that Google has actually
built their infrastructure

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:03.030
applications.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:03.780
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:08.380
So you said two words
that I almost understood.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:11.740
At least I knew one
of them was ASIC,

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:16.980
which we used before once,
which it is Application-Specific

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:18.800
Integrated Circuit.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.510
You also said FPA?

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.509
INES ENVID: Oh, FPA.

00:09:22.509 --> 00:09:23.050
It's similar.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.320
It's hardware purpose-built
for networking functions

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.394
where you actually do
lookups for packets.

00:09:28.394 --> 00:09:29.060
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:29.560
Interesting.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:32.480
INES ENVID: You
apply firewall rules.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.340
So it is really a very efficient
way of processing packets

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.680
that it was necessary,
really, in the past

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.250
in order to provide good
performance for networking

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:41.792
capabilities.

00:09:41.792 --> 00:09:42.708
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:42.708 --> 00:09:44.450
I was wondering if
you could discuss

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:47.010
one of the-- every
single time someone talks

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.010
about Google networking,
there is always

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:51.870
Jupiter that comes
out, like, that really

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:56.080
cool and powerful
magic, basically, thing.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.340
What is that exactly and
why is it so important?

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.060
INES ENVID: So
Jupiter is actually

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:09.320
just a fabric that is really
providing high capabilities

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:11.640
in terms of bandwidth
and performance,

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:16.590
and it really is built by having
very flexible modules that

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.120
communicate with each other.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.440
And modularizing that and
being able to scale it

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.430
provides a way for communicating
data centers in a way

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:27.380
that we don't have
bottlenecks in the network.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:31.560
So it is just basically a
highly scalable architecture

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.720
for networking.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Just that.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:34.690
INES ENVID: Just that.

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:36.481
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No,
that's pretty amazing.

00:10:36.481 --> 00:10:39.070
The fact that the network
is not the bottleneck,

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:40.530
that is pretty impressive.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:41.030
Cool.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:42.550
So it is really
impressive that we

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.650
have all of that
amazing infrastructure

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:47.490
to build applications on top
by running a Google Cloud

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:48.330
platform.

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:54.230
But from a more pragmatical
point of view, what can I do?

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:56.520
Like, if I have my
virtual machines, right?

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.960
My virtual machines, I
just start them and they

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:00.560
can communicate to each other.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:01.970
What else should I do?

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:02.720
INES ENVID: Right.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:06.110
So let me then get
started, now that we've

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:10.950
talked a little bit about the
infrastructure, the first thing

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:13.230
that customers can actually,
or users can leverage

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:14.670
on the infrastructure
is the fact

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:17.041
that that network is global.

00:11:17.041 --> 00:11:21.600
Global mean, we at Google have
our own backbone infrastructure

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:23.600
with the dedicated links.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:27.080
So it is our own
network-- actually,

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:29.210
there's two of
them-- that are just

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:32.470
dedicated for Google to
communicate their data center.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:37.760
So based on that, users
can build their sandboxes

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:41.520
with a global scope, which is
very unique in the industry,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.940
because you can have your
private RFC 1918 space, which

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:50.990
is the private IP space,
spanning all around the globe.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.660
So you can do very
unique things,

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:57.610
like having database
replication across regions

00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:00.760
or providing things like
global load balancing, which

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:03.300
allows you to have your
back-ends distributed

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:06.580
across regions for
redundancy purposes.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.710
And we provide some
very unique technologies

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:14.990
to have a single [INAUDIBLE]
for load balancing

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.670
that it sends you
via a technology

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.840
that we call Anycast
to the closest cluster

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:21.721
where you have
your applications.

00:12:21.721 --> 00:12:23.970
So that actually provides
you the best of both worlds.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:27.240
A very global scope,
while when you're actually

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.500
making a request, it
sends you to the closest

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:31.650
point for latency purposes.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.980
So the first thing is global.

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:37.520
Second thing is
you start building

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:39.980
your security on top of that.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:41.502
Once you have decided
where you need

00:12:41.502 --> 00:12:43.710
to have your applications,
that you need to make sure

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:44.915
that they're secure.

00:12:44.915 --> 00:12:47.310
And in order for
them to make secure,

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:50.340
your build your
firewalls on top of that.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:53.860
Those are all networking
functions that are implemented.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:57.410
Going back to the first
part of the conversation,

00:12:57.410 --> 00:12:59.950
they are built at the
host level, so truly,

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:04.080
truly distributed so that they
don't provide any bottlenecks

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:07.530
to your network while they're
securing your applications

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:10.220
right at the point
where they live,

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.920
so that you can set up
your firewalls for how

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:15.970
those applications
can communicate.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:20.860
Your user interface is [? do ?]
applied at your network level,

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:23.700
but when it actually
gets applied,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.990
then it goes to the host where
your applications are living,

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:29.500
so they get secure
at that same point.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:32.450
So you have your global network.

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:35.120
You have your firewalls
built on top of that, that

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:37.050
will give you your security.

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:41.700
Then you need to connect to
the rest of your application.

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:47.310
So most of the use cases will
involve having to transition,

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.460
migrate from existing
capabilities.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:52.610
So you need things like
VPN, which we fully

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:56.700
manage as well, as a service,
in order to connect securely

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:00.660
with your on-premises and you
can federate your environment.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.990
So it is really
great starting point

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:04.790
for building your sandbox.

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:07.660
And then on top of that,
there's plenty of applications

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:10.530
that we can talk
about, but that is

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:14.710
kind of the topology
plumbing, security aspect

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:17.570
of your sandbox, where
you can then just

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:18.760
build anything you need.

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:19.676
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

00:14:19.676 --> 00:14:20.820
That sounds great.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:23.870
But I know we have a variety
of tools and products,

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:26.659
like VPNs, you were talking
about, load balancers,

00:14:26.659 --> 00:14:28.450
but as well as a whole
bunch of other stuff

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:32.150
that I, personally, don't know a
huge amount about what they do.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:34.800
So why don't we take
a practical example?

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:36.680
So we have a web server.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:39.330
It wants to talk to the outside
internet, so that's great,

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:41.610
but it also talks to a
database maybe hosted

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.320
on a VM or something,
but we don't

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.230
want that database
to be accessible

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:46.530
to the outside internet.

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:48.900
How would I set that up
using our networking tools?

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.010
INES ENVID: Oh, that's
a great example.

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:56.900
So first, you want to
make sure that you really

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:58.960
don't have access
to the internet

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:05.460
from the applications or the
VMs that they don't have to.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:07.610
And the first thing
you can do, you

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:10.170
can actually protect that
with different layer,

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:11.950
which is always a good idea.

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:13.620
I think, first is,
you want to make sure

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.600
that you have firewall rules
that are actually not allowing

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:20.530
for any connections that
are coming from the outside

00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:22.560
to actually reach that machine.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:27.880
So we provide what we call
stateful firewalls, which

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:30.930
what it does is, really,
you set up a rule which

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.760
applies to the whole session.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:37.330
So as soon as the
session requests comes,

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:41.180
if it matches a certain
pattern, then you allow or deny.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:43.680
And then if you actually
deny that session,

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.560
like there's no
more chances for any

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:50.470
of their following up packets of
that session to actually enter.

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:55.130
So that provides a very easy
way of setting up the firewall.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.550
So you can set up
other networking level

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:58.650
those type of rules.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:00.350
And again, they could
apply to your host

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:01.730
where your database are living.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:06.060
So it is, as well,
very scalable,

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:08.344
where you're actually
applying those.

00:16:08.344 --> 00:16:10.010
The other thing that
you can actually do

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:15.270
is enforce that those
[? project ?] actually

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:21.610
doesn't have the capability of
having VMs with external IPs

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:22.110
at all.

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:25.060
So that will be an enforcement,
not so much on the user,

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:27.330
but it's actually trying
to change the behavior,

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:30.140
but at the project
level, so that regardless

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:35.426
of which role someone is using,
the enforcement, it is done.

00:16:35.426 --> 00:16:36.800
So that will be
for the database.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:39.040
And then obviously,
for your front-ends,

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:41.190
you have the
capabilities of pinging

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:44.170
just specifically the ports
for the web application

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:49.470
or specifically the ports
that you are trying to reach.

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:52.500
And if you're using capabilities
like HTTP load balancing,

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:55.750
then we provide
the proxy solution,

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:58.460
which, again, is the same that
Google uses for production,

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:00.490
so the front-ends are
the same and there

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:03.470
is very rich capabilities, for
instance, for DOS protection

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:05.400
in order to find
patterns that could

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:09.780
be possible attacks
for your application.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:14.319
So if you're using Google's
load balancing as a service,

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:17.530
you get advantage, by default,
by all those rich capabilities

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:18.910
that are already there.

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:19.826
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:17:19.826 --> 00:17:24.660
So what you're saying is that
if I have a bunch of instances

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.800
that are running
my web server, just

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:30.500
by putting a load
balancer in front of them

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.980
to facilitate the access and
share just one single IP,

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:37.107
I'm also gaining that protection
against denial of service

00:17:37.107 --> 00:17:38.190
and these kind of attacks.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:38.880
INES ENVID: That's correct.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:39.040
And--

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:39.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:41.540
INES ENVID: --those
capabilities are, again,

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:45.350
the same that apply
to the external access

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:50.450
communication that happens for
any of the Google applications,

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:54.105
so leveraging on those existing
capabilities is a great asset.

00:17:54.105 --> 00:17:55.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:56.760
What about VPCs?

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.590
I've heard about VPCs
