if you have more granularity
that cannot be expressed with

00:23:54.410 --> 00:23:58.170
IP ranges, you still have
the possibility of doing that

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:01.200
by applying metadata to the
specific instances that you

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:03.150
want to secure the
communication with.

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:05.510
So it gives you
yet another layer

00:24:05.510 --> 00:24:08.540
of flexibility on
top of the IP ranges

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:10.540
on how you actually
express your policies.

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:11.290
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:11.970
Cool.

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:14.550
So we talk a lot about, at
Google Cloud, particularly,

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:15.860
hybrid cloud.

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:19.350
Officially, on-prem with
cloud providers, networking

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:21.140
would seem like that's
a big part of that,

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:22.830
making sure we have
secure communication

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:24.670
between your on-premise
systems and what

00:24:24.670 --> 00:24:26.115
we have running in the cloud.

00:24:26.115 --> 00:24:28.210
What do we have that
enables us to do that?

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:29.685
How would that work?

00:24:29.685 --> 00:24:30.930
How does that happen?

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:33.610
INES ENVID: Yeah, so
there's many different ways

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:36.650
of communicating
with on-premises.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:40.890
Usually, when you're
communicating privately

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:44.050
to on-premises, you want to
have some security as well.

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:46.930
So once you actually
cross the internet, if you

00:24:46.930 --> 00:24:49.057
have certain applications
living in Google Cloud

00:24:49.057 --> 00:24:50.890
and you still have
certain applications that

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:54.030
live on your infrastructure
and your premises,

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:55.860
once you start
communicating them,

00:24:55.860 --> 00:25:00.250
you obviously need to go through
internet to build that bridge.

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:03.110
So there are several techniques.

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:06.670
One of the most popular
is to use IPsec tunnels,

00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:11.910
so BPS in order to provide
that secure communication.

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:15.790
So we offer completely
managed VPN building

00:25:15.790 --> 00:25:20.770
on top of IPsec that can connect
your BPC, your sandbox, so

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:22.940
specifically your
sandbox then, to make

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:25.240
sure it's only your
application who can actually

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:27.916
talk with your on-premises.

00:25:27.916 --> 00:25:31.630
And we provide capabilities
of up to a [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:35.840
per second IPsec tunnels that
can be actually horizontally

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:40.930
scaled, so that you can actually
build bandwidth on top of that.

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:46.580
There are other ways of
delivering that communication

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.650
between prem and Google.

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:52.809
And there are many
applications these days,

00:25:52.809 --> 00:25:54.600
as well, like the more
modern applications,

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:56.890
they actually have
application-level security.

00:25:56.890 --> 00:26:00.530
So they usually have SSL as
part of their application

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:01.200
communication.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.070
So some of the
users might decide

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:06.860
that they don't need
the transport security,

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:09.350
and they use the
application instead.

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:13.630
Doing that, you can just have
a regular pairing agreement

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:18.790
with Google or you can actually,
if you are a smaller user,

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:24.450
you can pair with any carrier,
so that you don't actually

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:29.397
need to maintain your own
ASN and there's a [? node ?]

00:26:29.397 --> 00:26:30.480
that you need to maintain.

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:35.120
So pairing with Google comes
with certain requirements.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:37.970
So for smaller users, you
just compare with any carrier.

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:42.400
We have plenty of agreements and
have that communication plane.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:45.950
And then on top of that, you can
either use IPsec, as we talked,

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:48.310
or you can build your
applications secure

00:26:48.310 --> 00:26:50.750
and just communicate
through SSL.

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:53.240
MARK MANDEL: We also have this
thing called Cloud Router.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:56.740
I don't understand
what it does, but it

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:58.780
seems to be doing, like,
kind of related to what

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:01.880
it is that we do here, in
terms of talking about on-prem,

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:05.424
because it does do stuff over
Cloud VPN, but I-- like I said,

00:27:05.424 --> 00:27:06.840
this networking
stuff confuses me.

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:09.510
So I'm very happy here and I
can ask really dumb questions.

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:10.220
INES ENVID: Yeah.

00:27:10.220 --> 00:27:14.230
So once you communicate
to on-premises,

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:18.710
you have certain workloads that
get represented by prefixes

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:22.070
in your VPC and then you
have certain applications

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:27.050
that get represented by
prefixes in your on-premises.

00:27:27.050 --> 00:27:30.100
So in order for
those two prefixes

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.130
to actually be able
to reach each other--

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:33.520
MARK MANDEL: What do
you mean by prefixes?

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:33.980
INES ENVID: IP.

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:34.440
MARK MANDEL: Oh, OK.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:34.939
Got it.

00:27:34.939 --> 00:27:37.690
INES ENVID: IP ranges.

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:41.470
So in order for those IP ranges
to be able to reach each other,

00:27:41.470 --> 00:27:44.315
you need to set up routes
and then you need to say,

00:27:44.315 --> 00:27:49.770
my IP range 1 needs to
be sent to my IP range 2,

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.390
and the other way around.

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:55.310
So you could always
hard-code that

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:59.110
and say, like, all the traffic
destined to my IP range 2,

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:01.976
I am going to send it with
the next hub, this tunnel,

00:28:01.976 --> 00:28:03.600
then you're done,
But then what happens

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:07.010
if you then add an IP range 3?

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:08.890
Then you need to
go and remember you

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:10.640
need to configure
another route, otherwise

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:12.270
you won't be able
to communicate.

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:16.600
So what Cloud Router provides
is dynamic advertisement

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:18.880
of those routes,
so then those can

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:22.630
be seen from on-premises as
reachable through the tunnel,

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:26.100
so then you don't really
need to worry about reaching

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.900
your applications across.

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:30.720
So it is built with
BEP capabilities

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:34.030
or dynamic routing
protocol, and then it

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:38.450
allows you to have a lot more
automated way to communicate

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:40.166
in your environments.

00:28:40.166 --> 00:28:41.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.040
So I have one last
question, which

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:48.010
is, OK, so I just
started-- I have

00:28:48.010 --> 00:28:50.310
my virtual machines running.

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:51.390
I just started them.

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:54.320
And I am running, whatever,
Fortress or something

00:28:54.320 --> 00:28:54.820
like that.

00:28:54.820 --> 00:29:00.380
I have my database and
one single web server.

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:02.910
What is the first thing I
should take into account?

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:05.040
Or what is the
resource that I should

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:06.989
start reading to make
sure that what I'm doing

00:29:06.989 --> 00:29:08.780
is not incredibly wrong
and I'm going to be

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:10.390
hacked in less than a minute?

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:11.765
INES ENVID: So
you want to set up

00:29:11.765 --> 00:29:15.120
just a simple VM into your
network to run an applications.

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:16.900
So a very simple setup.

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:19.660
So first thing,
you start the VM.

00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:22.130
You don't configure
it with public IP,

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:23.500
if you don't really have to.

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:26.190
So just make it private.

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:27.680
When you configure
your VM, you're

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:31.370
going to have your
capability so,

00:29:31.370 --> 00:29:33.795
selecting whether you
want to actually locate

00:29:33.795 --> 00:29:36.170
a public IP or not.

00:29:36.170 --> 00:29:40.590
You want to add metadatas with
[INAUDIBLE] you want to set up

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:43.320
a specific rules for that
specific workload that

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:47.890
might be distinct from others
that ping your own subnetwork.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:50.240
So you configure those on
your VM, because then based on

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:51.970
that, you can actually
set up policies

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:54.200
that get associated to that.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:58.130
Then you make it
part of a subnetwork.

00:29:58.130 --> 00:30:03.240
So your subnetwork is
going to define from which

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:04.770
IP range it gets located.

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:06.240
Is it private?

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:08.400
And you're pretty much
set up for your VM.

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:13.810
Now what you need to do is to
start building your firewall

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:14.880
rules on top of that.

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:17.880
So what you're
going to say is now

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:22.020
set that this specific VM
can only receive traffic

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:27.490
from source 1111, because
that is a trusted application,

00:30:27.490 --> 00:30:29.490
while all the rest, I'll block.

00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:33.032
So actually, by default,
we block all the traffic.

00:30:33.032 --> 00:30:34.830
And if you want to
talk with anyone,

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:36.790
you need to actually
go and open it

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:40.067
up so that you can talk
with your application.

00:30:40.067 --> 00:30:41.400
So you will build that firewall.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:45.670
You can say, apply only to this
label, which is the metadata

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:49.293
that you set up in your VM.

00:30:49.293 --> 00:30:52.570
And at that point, you are
pretty much up and running.

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:53.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:55.515
We are pretty much
running out of time.

00:30:55.515 --> 00:30:56.848
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, very much so.

00:30:56.848 --> 00:30:57.840
In fact--

00:30:57.840 --> 00:30:59.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
is there anything

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:02.320
else, that you would like to
mention something, some topic

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:04.280
that we might have forgotten?

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:05.330
INES ENVID: Not really.

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:06.920
I think one of
the things that we

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:10.840
are most proud of with
Google networking is really

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:15.140
the amazing infrastructure
that is sitting behind.

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:19.520
So it is the performance,
the type of throughput

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:22.050
that we have for
communicating to VMs.

00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:23.930
Like, for instance,
within a cluster,

00:31:23.930 --> 00:31:28.520
we can have up to 14
kilobit per second,

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:30.790
which is pretty amazing.

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:35.260
The latency, we can reach
