00:33:27.470 --> 00:33:30.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So to
finish with the episode,

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:32.430
we have one more
network thing, which

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:34.575
is the question of the week.

00:33:34.575 --> 00:33:36.100
Is it coming from Luna?

00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:38.810
MARK MANDEL: It is coming from
Luna, one of the good people

00:33:38.810 --> 00:33:40.640
who hang out on our Slack
channel for Google Cloud.

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:42.056
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, she's cool.

00:33:42.056 --> 00:33:42.990
Hello, Luna.

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:46.320
So yeah, she sent a question
that I did not understand much

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:50.660
about it, but to be honest, I
actually did not know, but OK.

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:53.170
So there's the thing that
some of you might know,

00:33:53.170 --> 00:33:57.100
which is so you have a request
that-- an HTTP request that

00:33:57.100 --> 00:33:59.370
goes through a proxy-- ah,
sorry, through a proxy,

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:01.160
through a load
balancer, and when

00:34:01.160 --> 00:34:04.405
you get it, the IP of the
packet that you receive actually

00:34:04.405 --> 00:34:08.980
is the origin is the load
balancer, not the customer that

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:10.362
actually sent you the request.

00:34:10.362 --> 00:34:11.820
But there is this
thing that allows

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:15.560
you to know what is the
actual origin, which

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:21.670
is included as a header in the
request, and that is for HTTP.

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:22.913
But what about TCP?

00:34:22.913 --> 00:34:25.639
TCP does not have headers?

00:34:25.639 --> 00:34:26.336
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:34:26.336 --> 00:34:27.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
how do you do that?

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:29.626
MARK MANDEL: So it's
an interesting one.

00:34:29.626 --> 00:34:32.076
So say you're-- classic example
is you're building a game

00:34:32.076 --> 00:34:34.159
or something, like you
have a WebSocket connection

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:36.310
or something that is
purely TCP-based, right?

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:37.730
You're not doing HTTP.

00:34:37.730 --> 00:34:40.900
But you want to know
where your originating IP

00:34:40.900 --> 00:34:42.929
address is from and,
again, same issue, right?

00:34:42.929 --> 00:34:44.387
Your network load
balancer is going

00:34:44.387 --> 00:34:46.460
to be the one that's the
IP you're going to see.

00:34:46.460 --> 00:34:49.659
So exactly like you would have,
say, an X-Forwarded header

00:34:49.659 --> 00:34:52.620
with HTTP, there's a thing
called the Proxy Protocol

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:54.060
that is built exactly for this.

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:56.639
So the Proxy Protocol we'll
have in the show notes.

00:34:56.639 --> 00:34:59.810
You can read all about it, but
it's basically a convenient way

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:02.520
to safely transport sort
of connection information

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:04.560
across a TCP connection.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:09.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I can use
that on my traditional TCP load

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:10.250
balancer?

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:12.249
MARK MANDEL: So that's
the interesting question.

00:35:12.249 --> 00:35:13.710
So we have a network
load balancer.

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:17.380
Unfortunately, it doesn't
support the Proxy Protocol.

00:35:17.380 --> 00:35:19.380
That's the standard network
load balancer, which

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:21.340
is a regional load balancer.

00:35:21.340 --> 00:35:25.610
But we do have a TCP
SSL load balancer.

00:35:25.610 --> 00:35:27.530
That's a load balancer
that will terminate

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:29.130
an SSL connection,
which you should

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:30.700
be using, because SSL, good.

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:30.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: SSL--

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:31.450
MARK MANDEL: Secure, yay.

00:35:31.450 --> 00:35:33.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--is always good.

00:35:33.250 --> 00:35:35.125
MARK MANDEL: And not
only is it not regional,

00:35:35.125 --> 00:35:36.750
it is global, which
is also great.

